        "title": "Introduction",
        "key_prerequisites": [],
        "has_content_in_snapshot": true,
        "sections": [
          {
            "section_type": "content",
            "normalized_html": "<p>As we know already, to evaluate numerical expressions with a mix of different operations, we follow the order of operations (PEMDAS).</p>",
            "readable_text": "As we know already, to evaluate numerical expressions with a mix of different operations, we follow the order of operations (PEMDAS).",
            "images": [],
            "free_entry_blanks": [],
            "select_lists": []
          },
          {
            "section_type": "content",
            "normalized_html": "<p>The same rules apply to numerical expressions with zero and negative exponents. To demonstrate, let&#x27;s compute the value of\n<span class=\"math\">[MATH: (-3)^0 +2^2 \\div 2.]</span></p>",
            "readable_text": "The same rules apply to numerical expressions with zero and negative exponents. To demonstrate, let's compute the value of\n[MATH: (-3)^0 +2^2 \\div 2.]",
            "images": [],
            "free_entry_blanks": [],
            "select_lists": []
          },
          {
            "section_type": "content",
            "normalized_html": "<p>To evaluate the given expression, we use PEMDAS.</p>",
            "readable_text": "To evaluate the given expression, we use PEMDAS.",
            "images": [],
            "free_entry_blanks": [],
            "select_lists": []
          },
          {
            "section_type": "content",
            "normalized_html": "<p>Notice that <span class=\"math\">[MATH: \\left(-3\\right)^{0}=1.]</span> So, we obtain</p>",
            "readable_text": "Notice that [MATH: \\left(-3\\right)^{0}=1.] So, we obtain",
            "images": [],
            "free_entry_blanks": [],
            "select_lists": []
          },
          {
            "section_type": "content",
            "normalized_html": "<p><span class=\"math\">[MATH: (\u2212 3)^{0} + 2^{2} \u00f7 2|=; 1 + 2^{2} \u00f7 2|=; 1 + 4 \u00f7 2|=; 1 + 2|=; 3|.]</span></p>",
            "readable_text": "[MATH: (\u2212 3)^{0} + 2^{2} \u00f7 2|=; 1 + 2^{2} \u00f7 2|=; 1 + 4 \u00f7 2|=; 1 + 2|=; 3|.]",
            "images": [],
            "free_entry_blanks": [],
            "select_lists": []
          }
        ],
        "images": [],
        "free_entry_blanks": [],
        "select_lists": [],
        "readable_text": "Content:\nAs we know already, to evaluate numerical expressions with a mix of different operations, we follow the order of operations (PEMDAS).\n\nContent:\nThe same rules apply to numerical expressions with zero and negative exponents. To demonstrate, let's compute the value of\n[MATH: (-3)^0 +2^2 \\div 2.]\n\nContent:\nTo evaluate the given expression, we use PEMDAS.\n\nContent:\nNotice that [MATH: \\left(-3\\right)^{0}=1.] So, we obtain\n\nContent:\n[MATH: (\u2212 3)^{0} + 2^{2} \u00f7 2|=; 1 + 2^{2} \u00f7 2|=; 1 + 4 \u00f7 2|=; 1 + 2|=; 3|.]"
      },
