          },
          {
            "section_type": "example_explanation",
            "normalized_html": "<p>To evaluate the given expression, we use PEMDAS.</p><p>Any number raised to the power of <span class=\"math\">[MATH: 0]</span> equals <span class=\"math\">[MATH: 1.]</span> So, we have </p><p><span class=\"math\">[MATH: \\left( 1.5 + \\dfrac{2}{15} \\right) ^0 = 1.]</span></p><p>Next, we evaluate the negative exponent and get</p><p><span class=\"math\">[MATH: \\left(-4\\right)^{-2}  = \\dfrac{1}{(-4)^2} = \\dfrac{1}{16}.]</span></p><p>Substituting all these values into the expression, we obtain:</p><p><span class=\"math\">[MATH: 2 + (\u2212 4)^{\u2212 2} \u00f7 (1.5 + (2)/(15))^{0}|=; 2 + (1)/(16) \u00f7 1|=; 2 + (1)/(16)|=; (32)/(16) + (1)/(16)|=; (33)/(16)]</span></p>",
            "readable_text": "To evaluate the given expression, we use PEMDAS.\n\nAny number raised to the power of [MATH: 0] equals [MATH: 1.] So, we have\n\n[MATH: \\left( 1.5 + \\dfrac{2}{15} \\right) ^0 = 1.]\n\nNext, we evaluate the negative exponent and get\n\n[MATH: \\left(-4\\right)^{-2} = \\dfrac{1}{(-4)^2} = \\dfrac{1}{16}.]\n\nSubstituting all these values into the expression, we obtain:\n\n[MATH: 2 + (\u2212 4)^{\u2212 2} \u00f7 (1.5 + (2)/(15))^{0}|=; 2 + (1)/(16) \u00f7 1|=; 2 + (1)/(16)|=; (32)/(16) + (1)/(16)|=; (33)/(16)]",
            "images": [],
            "free_entry_blanks": [],
