    "readable_text": "Item 1 \u2014 Tutorial: Introduction\n\nContent:\nAs we know already, to evaluate numerical expressions with a mix of different operations, we follow the order of operations (PEMDAS).\n\nContent:\nThe same rules apply to numerical expressions with zero and negative exponents. To demonstrate, let's compute the value of\n[MATH: (-3)^0 +2^2 \\div 2.]\n\nContent:\nTo evaluate the given expression, we use PEMDAS.\n\nContent:\nNotice that [MATH: \\left(-3\\right)^{0}=1.] So, we obtain\n\nContent:\n[MATH: (\u2212 3)^{0} + 2^{2} \u00f7 2|=; 1 + 2^{2} \u00f7 2|=; 1 + 4 \u00f7 2|=; 1 + 2|=; 3|.]\n\n---\n\nItem 2 \u2014 Example: Evaluating Expressions With Zero Exponents\n\nExample Prompt:\nCalculate [MATH: 10\\div 5^0 + (3^2-5).]\n\nContent:\nEXPLANATION\n\nExplanation:\nTo evaluate the given expression, we use PEMDAS.\n\nFirst, we compute the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n[MATH: 10 \u00f7 5^{0} + (9 \u2212 5) = 10 \u00f7 5^{0} + 4]\n\nNext, notice that [MATH: 5^0=1.] So, we obtain:\n\n[MATH: 10 \u00f7 5^{0} + 4|=; 10 \u00f7 1 + 4|=; 10 + 4|=; 14]\n\n---\n\nItem 3 \u2014 Question 1\n\nPrompt:\n[MATH: 8^{2} \u00f7 16 + 11^{0} =]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: 4]\nb. [MATH: 8]\nc. [MATH: 15]\nd. [MATH: 17]\ne. [MATH: 5]\n\n---\n\nItem 4 \u2014 Question 2\n\nPrompt:\n[MATH: \u2212 (3)^{2} + (\u2212 5) \u00d7 2^{0} =]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: \u2212 1]\nb. [MATH: \u2212 4]\nc. [MATH: 4]\nd. [MATH: \u2212 14]\ne. [MATH: 1]\n\n---\n\nItem 5 \u2014 Example: Evaluating Expressions With Negative Exponents\n\nExample Prompt:\nWhat is the value of [MATH: \\dfrac{4}{3} \\times (-5+3)^{-2} + \\dfrac{2}{3} ?]\n\nContent:\nEXPLANATION\n\nExplanation:\nTo evaluate the given expression, we use PEMDAS.\n\nFirst, we compute the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n[MATH: \\dfrac{4}{3} \\times ({\\color{red}-5} + {\\color{red}3})^{-2} + \\dfrac{2}{3} = \\dfrac{4}{3} \\times ({\\color{red}-2})^{-2} + \\dfrac{2}{3}]\n\nNext, notice that [MATH: (-2)^{-2}=\\dfrac{1}{(-2)^2} = \\dfrac{1}{4}.] So, we obtain:\n\n[MATH: (4)/(3) \u00d7 (\u2212 2)^{\u2212 2} + (2)/(3)|=; (4)/(3) \u00d7 (1)/(4) + (2)/(3)|=; (4 \u00d7 1)/(3 \u00d7 4) + (2)/(3)|=; (4 \u00d7 1)/(3 \u00d7 4) + (2)/(3)|=; (1)/(3) + (2)/(3)|=; 1]\n\n---\n\nItem 6 \u2014 Question 3\n\nPrompt:\n[MATH: 5^{\u2212 2} \u00f7 (1)/(5) =]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: \u2212 25]\nb. [MATH: 5]\nc. [MATH: (1)/(25)]\nd. [MATH: \u2212 (1)/(5)]\ne. [MATH: (1)/(5)]\n\n---\n\nItem 7 \u2014 Question 4\n\nPrompt:\n[MATH: 2^{\u2212 2} \u00f7 ((1)/(4) \u2212 1) =]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: (1)/(6)]\nb. [MATH: \u2212 (1)/(3)]\nc. [MATH: (1)/(3)]\nd. [MATH: 3]\ne. [MATH: \u2212 6]\n\n---\n\nItem 8 \u2014 Example: Evaluating Expressions With Both Zero and Negative Exponents\n\nExample Prompt:\nFind the value of [MATH: 2 + \\left(-4\\right)^{-2} \\div \\left( 1.5 + \\dfrac{2}{15} \\right)^0.]\n\nContent:\nEXPLANATION\n\nExplanation:\nTo evaluate the given expression, we use PEMDAS.\n\nAny number raised to the power of [MATH: 0] equals [MATH: 1.] So, we have\n\n[MATH: \\left( 1.5 + \\dfrac{2}{15} \\right) ^0 = 1.]\n\nNext, we evaluate the negative exponent and get\n\n[MATH: \\left(-4\\right)^{-2} = \\dfrac{1}{(-4)^2} = \\dfrac{1}{16}.]\n\nSubstituting all these values into the expression, we obtain:\n\n[MATH: 2 + (\u2212 4)^{\u2212 2} \u00f7 (1.5 + (2)/(15))^{0}|=; 2 + (1)/(16) \u00f7 1|=; 2 + (1)/(16)|=; (32)/(16) + (1)/(16)|=; (33)/(16)]\n\n---\n\nItem 9 \u2014 Question 5\n\nPrompt:\nCalculate [MATH: ((5)/(8))^{0} \u00d7 (3.5 \u2212 1.5)^{\u2212 3}.]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: (1)/(8)]\nb. [MATH: \u2212 8]\nc. [MATH: 2]\nd. [MATH: (1)/(2)]\ne. [MATH: \u2212 (1)/(4)]\n\n---\n\nItem 10 \u2014 Question 6\n\nPrompt:\n[MATH: 18 \u00f7 ((1)/(3))^{\u2212 2} + ((1)/(101) \u2212 0.01)^{0} =]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: (3)/(2)]\nb. [MATH: 3]\nc. [MATH: 1]\nd. [MATH: 2]\ne. [MATH: (4)/(3)]"
  }
